---
title: "Cascade layers"
description: "Layer order decides what wins, so nothing in the kit needs specificity tricks."
section: Foundations
sectionOrder: 1
order: 4
---

The kit declares its layers once, in `base/_layers.css`, and every stylesheet writes into one of them:

```css
@layer variables, reset, vendors, legacy, ui, overrides;

@layer legacy {
  @layer imports, components, utilities, migrations;
}

@layer ui {
  @layer components, plugins, utilities;
}
```

| Layer | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `variables` | tokens and every `--ui-*` hook default |
| `reset` | the element reset and the typography roles |
| `vendors` | third-party CSS you import (Embla, a map widget) |
| `legacy.*` | an existing app's stylesheets during a migration, in the order they used to cascade |
| `ui.components` | the primitives |
| `ui.plugins` | kit additions that extend a primitive |
| `ui.utilities` | style utilities and switches, at zero specificity |
| `overrides` | your rules; they beat everything above |

A later layer wins regardless of selector specificity, so a utility beats a primitive rule without `!important`, and a one-line rule in `overrides` beats a utility. Never import a stylesheet into bare `legacy` or `ui`: un-sublayered rules form an implicit final sublayer that outranks the named ones.

## Importing your own CSS

```css
@import "./brand.css" layer(overrides);
@import "./old-app.css" layer(legacy.imports);
```

Unlayered CSS outranks every layer, so the kit keeps nothing unlayered. Import a legacy stylesheet into `legacy.*` rather than leaving it bare; left bare, it keeps winning over the kit until you move the rules.
